a small gesture of thanks  one of the many agnieszka sophia shaq  has experienced since she took ukrainian  refugees into her home in poland  12 people now live in a space normally  meant for a family of four  [Music]  normally we'd extend this table at  christmas now it's pulled out all the  way all the time it's like it's always  christmas but at least this way we have  room for everyone  when war broke out in neighboring  ukraine agnieszka offered to  help wanted to make this room available  to three people but then in the middle  of the night a driver arrived with eight  people  the children were coughing and the women  started crying because they didn't want  to be split up  [Music]  as the war continues that act of  kindness has become a permanent solution  her family's exhausted  for two months now i haven't known what  it's like to come home to peace and  quiet  and perhaps we need that to start  getting back to normal  her sister and a friend arrived from  western ukraine with five children  they feel helpless and very  uncomfortable about it  this is their life  their family  they're used to being together  suddenly having eight more people around  is very hard  i'm even ashamed that aniesca and her  husband pay for all of our expenses  they buy everything the food and  everything we and the children need  to help the families that take in the  refugees the polish government plans to  make monthly payments of around 250  euros for each person they accommodate  but so far agnieszka has not received  anything yudmila tries to earn a little  something as a cleaning lady but can't  afford a place of her own  i have three children but even if i only  had myself to support  it would be very tough for me  many ukrainian refugees are in similarly  dire straits  they came to warsaw's national stadium  to register they're thankful for the  warm welcome but worry about their  future  i don't know what to do unfortunately  there is no work to be had here  poland has taken in lots of refugees  feeds them and keeps them warm  but there are just no apartments and no  jobs i want either to stay in poland and  get a job  or go to germany and find work there  you can't just sit around all the time  doing nothing people here give us their  last scent  yesterday for instance a woman donated  over a thousand dollars to our lodgings  they help a lot with everything we're  living here at their expense  rafael tamala has also been busy helping  ever since the war broke out he lets 20  ukrainian women and 40 children live at  his hostel in warsaw for free but he  also hopes for assistance from the state  you have enough strength and resources  for two more weeks  we're financing this from our own  private means because we haven't  received any financial help  we used to rent out rooms but we're not  taking any money from the refugees  with summer on the way many hotel owners  are faced with a dilemma let the  refugees stay on or take in paying  guests  given the expenses the polish state is  facing warsaw is hoping for more  financial aid from the eu  the european union's decision to provide  funding for refugees involves large sums  of money but it's still just a drop in  the bucket  it's very little  from the polish government's point of  view it would be important to set up a  fund to administer such it on behalf of  the eu and cover the needs of refugees  although she'd be glad to see at least a  little money soon agnieszka sobjershak  doesn't regret helping refugees  what's most important is that they don't  have bombs raining down on them we don't  need luxury  but with so many people here i must say  more aid is needed this is a bit much  but it's definitely been worth it  it seems most poles still feel the same  way  and like their ukrainian guests they  hope the war won't last much longer
 

